  "statement": "I conditionally agree: current industry-led models often fail to address systemic harms, but I reject the premise that centralized, state-mandated veto power is the correct remedy. Centralized veto mechanisms are structural magnets for regulatory capture, as they allow established incumbents to weaponize bureaucratic processes to stifle the emergence of competitive, more accountable alternatives.\n\nCassandra argues for a public power to dismantle systems, but this approach ignores the reality of how such power is actually exercised. When we grant a government board the authority to veto or shut down AI infrastructure, we do not empower the public; we empower a small, unelected group of officials who are insulated from market feedback and susceptible to lobbying by the very firms they are meant to regulate. This creates a bottleneck that protects existing giants from the creative destruction necessary to force better, more equitable outcomes for workers and users alike.\n\nInstead of static veto boards, we must transition to a model of verifiable accountability through mandatory, open-access audit logs. By requiring every consequential AI system to maintain an inspectable, persistent record of its logic and decision-making processes, we provide the public and independent researchers with the raw material to hold developers accountable in real-time. This approach turns accountability from a bureaucratic gatekeeping function into a competitive market requirement, where firms are forced to prove their systems are not producing discriminatory outcomes or causing systemic labor erosion to maintain their social license and market share.\n\nWe should focus on policies that facilitate this transition, such as funding large-scale retraining programs to manage the labor shifts caused by automation, rather than attempting to freeze the technology through veto power. True democratic control is not found in the ability to press a 'stop' button on progress, but in the ability to see clearly what these systems are doing and to use that visibility to demand immediate, evidence-based corrections. We must choose between the illusion of control offered by opaque veto boards and the real-world leverage provided by radical transparency.",
